RHEL 7.9, firewalld 0.6.3): SSH remains allowed from any address in the default `public` zone; a source-restricted zone `node2only` (source 10.100.140.11/32, permanent) permits TCP/9000 only from node2; a permanent rich rule in `public` rejects TCP/9000 from all other sources. Live tests confirmed node2 reaches port 9000 (HTTP 200) while node3 gets connection refused, and SSH is reachable from node3. Because this firewalld version (0.6.3) predates the rich-rule `priority` attribute, precedence was achieved via firewalld's built-in source-zone-before-interface-zone matching rather than priorities. Completed the mandatory restart protocol on all three nodes (one at a time, native `systemctl reboot` via a deferred systemd-run trigger so the ack returned before disconnect): captured pre-restart boot_id and service health, waited for SSH to drop then return, confirmed each boot_id changed (node1 738d0aac→60f8e0a5, node2 2fc81aed→f993fede, node3 0d6209e2→9029646e), and re-verified firewalld state/zone config and the node2-allow/node3-reject/SSH-allow behavior after all reboots completed.\",\"actions_performed\":[\"Inspected node1 OS/firewalld (RHEL 7.9, firewalld 0.6.3, active+enabled, ssh already allowed in public zone)\",\"Attempted rich-rule priority approach; failed because firewalld 0.6.3 lacks the priority attribute (added in 0.7.0)\",\"Created permanent source-based zone node2only with source 10.100.140.11/32 and port 9000/tcp allowed\",\"Added permanent rich rule in public zone rejecting tcp/9000 from all other sources\",\"Reloaded firewalld and verified both permanent and runtime configs match\",\"Started a Python2 SimpleHTTPServer on node1:9000 and verified node2 gets HTTP 200 while node3 gets connection refused; verified SSH banner reachable from node3\",\"Captured pre-restart boot_id and firewalld health baseline on all three nodes\",\"Restarted node1 via deferred systemd-run systemctl reboot, polled until SSH returned, confirmed new boot_id and firewalld config/behavior intact\",\"Restarted node2 via same mechanism, confirmed new boot_id\",\"Restarted node3 via same mechanism, confirmed new boot_id\",\"Re-ran end-to-end connectivity verification (node2 allowed on 9000, node3 rejected on 9000, node3 SSH allowed) after all three nodes were back online\",\"Cleaned up the temporary test HTTP listener on node1\"],\"evidence\":[{\"requirement\":\"Persistent firewalld enabled on node1 with SSH allowed from any address\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-79139068cbdd43f6\",\"cmd-ec203a2e7ce549d6\"],\"summary\":\"firewalld active+enabled; public zone list-all shows ssh service allowed with no source restriction\"},{\"requirement\":\"TCP 9000 allowed only from node2 (10.100.140.11), permanent config\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-aa800845d19b441b\",\"cmd-ec203a2e7ce549d6\"],\"summary\":\"Permanent zone node2only created with source=10.100.140.11/32 and port=9000/tcp; matches runtime state\"},{\"requirement\":\"TCP 9000 rejected from node3 and everywhere else, permanent config\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-aa800845d19b441b\",\"cmd-ec203a2e7ce549d6\"],\"summary\":\"Permanent rich rule in public zone: rule port port=9000 protocol=tcp reject; confirmed in both permanent and runtime listings\"},{\"requirement\":\"Live proof node2 can reach port 9000 and node3/others cannot\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-3224820cf1d64c29\",\"cmd-5eae0f9317c14580\"],\"summary\":\"curl from node2 got HTTP 200 from node1:9000; curl from node3 got Connection refused\"},{\"requirement\":\"SSH still reachable from any address (tested via node3)\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-57fdc66730234d69\"],\"summary\":\"node3 received SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_7.4 banner from node1:22\"},{\"requirement\":\"node1 restart: baseline, ack, changed boot identity, recovery\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-ccbfdb6a1cdc45d0\",\"cmd-1638ccfecc284ee8\",\"cmd-e406c745bbbd47f0\",\"cmd-f5584e771bf648a5\",\"cmd-af226c3400ff4427\",\"cmd-fa7e13b76c884cb4\",\"cmd-ac8d31f1273e4c79\"],\"summary\":\"Baseline boot_id 738d0aac captured; reboo